{
	title: 'OpenAI Responses over WebSocket',
	spec: "OpenAI requests go over a Responses WebSocket per session, as the Codex CLI does (codex-rs/core/src/client.rs): header OpenAI-Beta responses_websockets=2026-02-06 plus the HTTP headers, messages {type: 'response.create', ...the HTTP body}. When a request's non-input fields equal the last completed one's and its input starts with that request's input followed by the reply's items, only the new items go, with previous_response_id; otherwise the full input. Server events become SSE bytes, so provider.ts keeps one parse, timeout and abort path (Provider.open). The socket is dropped on abort, timeout, close mid-response, error, failed or incomplete responses, previous_response_not_found and the 60-minute websocket_connection_limit_reached (the latter two retried as temporary with full input on a new socket), and after 10 idle minutes. A socket that fails to connect within 10 s sends that request and the next 10 minutes over HTTP. One request per socket at a time; a second concurrent one uses HTTP. Live probe 6 Oct: a 106k-token tool loop sent 730 bytes per follow-up request instead of 313 kB, same cache hits, no measurable latency gain from a datacenter host. Test: a continuation sends only the new item with previous_response_id, an edited earlier message sends everything, a refused upgrade falls back to HTTP.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"A socket that closes mid-response (pi names 1009, message too big) puts that session on HTTP for the host's lifetime, as pi-mono's openai-codex-responses.ts does; otherwise the temporary-failure retry would resend the same oversized message forever. pi-mono (checked 6 Oct) also defaults to WebSocket with per-session SSE fallback, retries previous_response_not_found and the connection limit once, sends session-id and x-client-request-id headers, and zstd-compresses HTTP bodies.",
		"Request bodies are compressed (HttpRequest.encoding, Bun.zstdCompressSync/gzipSync): the Codex backend gets zstd as the Codex CLI sends it (live: 34k tokens in 5.9 kB, cache hits unchanged); the Anthropic Messages API refuses zstd with a 400 but takes gzip (live: 193 kB in 82 kB, cache hits unchanged). pi-mono compresses only Codex. Bun's WebSocket client negotiates permessage-deflate with the Codex backend on its own, as the Codex CLI enables it. Fake servers in tests decompress like the real APIs."
	],
	needs: ['q7']
}
